{
  "about": "Pedestrian Dynamics body model: 16 capsule segments in the standing neutral pose. Positions and radii are fractions of stature H (x forward, y left, z up, origin on the ground between the feet); masses are percent of body mass. Joint angles in degrees from the neutral pose; gains and torques for the 75 kg, 1.75 m reference, scaled by mass x stature. Left-side segments name their mirrored right counterpart.",
  "sources": {
    "mass": "de Leva P. (1996) Adjustments to Zatsiorsky-Seluyanov's segment inertia parameters. J Biomech 29(9):1223-1230, male values (head 6.94, upper/middle/lower trunk 15.96/16.33/11.17, upper arm 2.71, forearm 1.62, hand 0.61, thigh 14.16, shank 4.33, foot 1.37 %). Read from the BMClab anthropometry tables (CC-BY-4.0).",
    "lengths": "Drillis R, Contini R (1966) Body segment parameters, as tabulated in Winter DA (2009) Biomechanics and Motor Control of Human Movement, Fig. 4.1: shoulder 0.818 H, elbow 0.630, wrist 0.485, fingertip 0.377, hip 0.530, knee 0.285, ankle 0.039, biacromial 0.259, hip width 0.191, foot length 0.152. Quoted from memory of the textbook figure; to be rechecked against the primary source.",
    "ranges": "Typical active ranges (AAOS 1965; Greene & Heckman 1994) from secondary sources: hip flexion 120, extension 20, abduction 45, adduction 25; knee 0-140; ankle dorsiflexion 20, plantarflexion 50; shoulder flexion 170 (capped short of 180 for the swing-twist split), extension 50, abduction 170; elbow 0-145; neck flexion 45, extension 55, rotation 70, lateral 40. Spine split across lumbar and thoracic joints. Passive end-of-range stiffness and ligament tear torques are estimates.",
    "gains": "Estimated. Same order as the DeepMimic / AMP humanoid PD gains (MIT / Apache-2.0) and Coros, Beaudoin, van de Panne (2010) Generalized Biped Walking Control; torque caps below typical maximum voluntary joint torques of an adult male.",
    "tissue": "Contact stiffness of each segment's soft tissue (series with the surface hit): estimates; the head's (scalp) gives ground and windscreen head contacts a duration of a few milliseconds."
  },
